# Line endings are pinned here so every file has ONE identity on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true by default. Without this file every .sh in the
# tree checks out CRLF on a Windows clone INCLUDING the shebang, and running one directly
# then fails with `/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': Permission denied` (rc 126) — the shebang names
# `env`, so `env` is what refuses, not the kernel. It is invisible in CI, which runs on
# ubuntu where the files stay LF, and invisible to `bash script.sh`, which bypasses the
# shebang entirely. That is how it survived three separate harnesses. See #95.
#
# It also gives one file two identities: scripts/test-release-packaging.sh reads md5
# 9998764d in a Windows checkout and 6fd8bf6a in a Linux one. Any gate comparing a script
# hash across the two sides reports a mismatch that is pure line-ending noise.
#
# NOTE for an existing Windows clone: `git pull` does not re-materialise files that are
# already checked out. Run `git add --renormalize .` or take a fresh clone, or your .sh
# stay CRLF and this file appears to have done nothing.

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf

# PowerShell is a Windows consumer and CRLF is what it expects — but it is pinned rather
# than left to autocrlf for the same reason as the others: unpinned means the file hashes
# one way on Windows and another on Linux, which is the trap above. Leaving one script
# family unpinned in the commit that pins the rest is how the next gap gets created.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
